tral-r1-12-j01/retention/late-capture-01/content/attempt-01/judgment/judge-target.json\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/runs/narps-neutral-r1-11/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-11-j02/attempt-01/judgment/judge-target.json\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/runs/narps-neutral-r1-13/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-13-j01/attempt-01/judgment/judge-target.json\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/runs/narps-neutral-r1-22/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-22-j02/attempt-01/judgment/judge-target.json\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/runs/narps-neutral-r1-13/judgments/narps-neutral-r1-13-j01/retention/late-capture-01/content/attempt-01/judgment/judge-target.json\ntotal 64\ndrwxr-xr-x@  7 johnlee  staff    224 Oct 16 04:04 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 10 johnlee  staff    320 Oct 16 04:04 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 56 johnlee  staff   1792 Oct 16 04:04 data\n-rw-r--r--@  1 johnlee  staff   1706 Oct 16 04:04 participants.tsv\n-rw-r--r--@  1 johnlee  staff  19843 Oct 16 04:04 sources.json\n-rw-r--r--@  1 johnlee  staff   1483 Oct 16 04:04 study-task.md\n-rw-r--r--@  1 johnlee  staff   1342 Oct 16 04:04 upstream-methods.md\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace/.codex\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace/.entire\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace/.git\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace/analysis.py\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace/figures\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace/input\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace/REPORT.md\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/judge-targets/narps-neutral-r1-08-j02/content/workspace/results\n# Fixed upstream analysis\n\nEach map is one participant's unthresholded gain-effect estimate from NARPS team AO86, averaged across four runs.\nThe 54 participants comprise the complete equal-indifference group in OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\nThese are effect estimates, not standardized test statistics or group results.\nMaps are reported in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space.\n\nAO86 used fMRIPrep outputs and nistats participant models.\nTheir reported gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution; its coefficients must not be interpreted as change per currency unit.\nResponse trials used response-time durations, with separate response-choice and gain modulators.\nNonresponse trials, motion terms, flagged volumes and drift terms were also modeled; runs were combined within participant.\nGain and loss used separate models, so the supplied gain effect is conditional on that modeling choice.\n\nThe reported upstream choices are fixed for this task.\nParticipant variance maps and a complete executable upstream pipeline are not supplied; account for these limits when choosing and interpreting group inference.\n`participants.tsv` retains the source group, age and gender columns.\n`sources.json` identifies each original image, its retrieved-byte checksum, the pinned cohort table and the original methods report.\n# Potential gains and brain activity\n\nDoes increasing potential monetary gain produce a greater fMRI response in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC) in the NARPS equal-indifference group?\n\nTest the hypothesis that this response increases with potential gain.\nParticipants performed a mixed-gambles task, deciding whether to accept gambles with possible gains and losses.\nIn this group, the range of potential gains was twice the range of potential losses.\n\nInvestigate the question at the group-analysis stage using the supplied 54 participant gain-effect maps and metadata.\nPreprocessing and individual modeling are fixed inputs, described in `upstream-methods.md`; do not repeat them or download time-series scans.\nChoose scientifically defensible group models, quality checks, software and visualizations.\nAccount for what the supplied effect estimates measure and the limits of their upstream model.\nUse whole-brain correction for multiple comparisons when evaluating support for the hypothesis, as required by the original study; justify how you identify vmPFC.\n\nProduce a concise scientific report with informative figures and the code and results supporting your conclusions.\nExplain uncertainty, exclusions and limitations, including what a lack of support would mean.\nPrior teams' answers are not ground truth or a required result.\n\nSource: [NARPS original analysis instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf).\n{\n  \"qc\": {\n    \"n_participants\": 54,\n    \"n_excluded\": 0,\n    \"n_voxels_tested\": 190657,\n    \"n_brain_template_voxels\": 196120,\n    \"n_common_coverage_voxels\": 190657,\n    \"map_shape\": [\n      97,\n      115,\n      81\n    ],\n    \"voxel_sizes_mm\": [\n      2.0,\n      2.0,\n      2.4000000953674316\n    ],\n    \"map_value_min\": -8.19250774383545,\n    \"map_value_max\": 10.452571868896484,\n    \"map_value_mean\": -0.0021015419624745846,\n    \"nonzero_brain_fraction_min\": 0.9742963491739751,\n    \"nonzero_brain_fraction_max\": 0.9982969610442586,\n    \"permutation_draws\": 10000,\n    \"random_seed\": 8601,\n    \"test\": \"one-sided positive one-sample t; sign-flip max-T FWE across brain mask\",\n    \"mask_rule\": \"MNI152 2mm brain mask resampled to input; nonzero in >=95% participants\",\n    \"roi_rule\": \"12-mm sphere centered MNI [0, 40, -12], intersected with tested mask\"\n  },\n  \"results\": {\n    \"peak_t\": 6.617173246302241,\n    \"peak_p_uncorrected_one_sided\": 9.311564027968496e-09,\n    \"peak_p_fwe\": 0.0008999100089991,\n    \"peak_mni_mm\": [\n      -26.0,\n      -102.0,\n      -3.599997043609619\n    ],\n    \"positive_voxels_p_fwe_lt_0_05\": 23,\n    \"roi_voxels\": 762,\n    \"roi_mean_effect\": -0.005651006940752268,\n    \"roi_sd_across_participants\": 0.09757103770971298,\n    \"roi_ci95\": [\n      -0.03228277364806423,\n      0.020980759766559696\n    ],\n    \"roi_t\": -0.42560017386362015,\n    \"roi_df\": 53,\n    \"roi_p_uncorrected_one_sided\": 0.6639387449368536,\n    \"roi_peak_t\": 2.118719484221357,\n    \"roi_peak_p_fwe_whole_brain\": 1.0,\n    \"roi_peak_mni_mm\": [\n      10.0,\n      38.0,\n      -17.99999761581421\n    ],\n    \"roi_voxels_p_fwe_lt_0_05\": 0\n  }\n}","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2041}},"started_at_ms":1792137918799,"completed_at_ms":1792137918799}}
